WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.327
This decision tree provides a simple reasoning process.

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:06.842
First, ask whether the values are labels or quantities.

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:10.537
If they are labels, ask whether a meaningful order exists.

00:00:10.537 --> 00:00:15.732
If they are quantities, ask whether the scale has a true zero.

00:00:15.732 --> 00:00:21.452
This framework works well for many common variables, but domain context still matters.

00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:24.962
When unsure, explain which comparisons or arithmetic statements would make real-world sense.

00:00:24.962 --> 00:00:32.192
The easiest way to classify a variable is to ask what statements are meaningful in the real world.

00:00:32.192 --> 00:00:38.952
Can values only be different, can they be ordered, are differences meaningful, and does zero mean absence?

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:44.332
Those questions are more reliable than looking at whether the software stores the column as an integer or

00:00:44.332 --> 00:00:46.946
floating-point number.
